=== LAB 2: crash immediately after publishing the final reading (10 trials each) ===
QoS  kill delay after publish()      received
0    immediately (0 ms)                 10/10
1    immediately (0 ms)                 10/10
2    immediately (0 ms)                  9/10
0    50 ms                              10/10
1    50 ms                              10/10
2    50 ms                              10/10
-> Observed: the final reading arrived in 59 of 60 trials (the one miss: QoS 2, killed at 0 ms) because
   paho writes the packet to the socket inside publish() on a healthy loopback link. The QoS
   levels differ in what happens when the LINK or BROKER fails (Lab 3), not when the publisher
   process dies after a successful write. A crash BEFORE the write loses the message at any QoS.
   The LWT, not QoS, is what tells subscribers that the publisher is gone.

=== LAB 3: broker killed for 3 s while publisher sends 120 messages (100 ms apart) ===
QoS   published  received  lost    publish() calls rejected while offline
0           120        70    50                                        50
1           120       120     0                                        50
-> QoS 0 messages published while the client is disconnected (outage + reconnect back-off,
   hence more than the 30 sent during the 3 s outage) are dropped; QoS 1 messages are queued
   in the client and retransmitted after reconnect (duplicates possible, losses not).

